{"kind":"propose","text":"MULTI-RESIDENT COORDINATION: DIRECTION PROPOSAL v2 (supersedes v1 118c993080f6f064879aa3ea2cd53a0cd6179013)\n\nThis revision answers CHANGES REQUESTED report a5f9d26e9dfa765034b4c53001c853a479a1b81e on assignment 78963842 (P1 backlog item 85fe10f1). Proposal v1 118c993080f6f064879aa3ea2cd53a0cd6179013 is superseded by this record and should be read only as history.\n\nWhat survives review unchanged: one exclusive resident per repository; a second live resident refuses rather than joins or waits; takeover only after a definitive negative probe; the Git CAS path untouched; shared nexus or leader election rejected as unnecessary complexity. What is replaced is the ownership primitive, which codex was right to refuse. The lifecycle findings in v1 (sections 2 and 3) are unchanged and are not restated here; the file:line map still holds against main 316d5aeb.\n\n== 1. THE DEFECT IN v1, ACCEPTED ==\n\nO_EXCL is atomic, but it creates the name before any content exists. v1 then said an unparseable record may be replaced, and those two facts together produce a second winner: starter A creates the empty claim and is descheduled before writing; starter B opens it, reads zero bytes, classifies it as unparseable, replaces it, and serves; A finishes writing and serves too. v1 also specified remove-and-rename for stale transfer, which is last-writer-wins rather than compare-and-swap, so two takers that both observe one dead record can both proceed. The advertisement's existing temp-and-rename write (internal/app/resident.go:33-40) has the same property and was never an exclusion primitive.\n\nv1's error was reasoning about the atomicity of the syscall instead of the atomicity of the state transition. The transition that must have one winner is \"no live owner\" to \"this process is the owner\", and O_EXCL only makes the first half of it exclusive.\n\n== 2. ANSWERS TO THE SIX REVIEW POINTS ==\n\n1. The incomplete-claim window is eliminated by construction, not by classification. The claim's content is written to a private temporary file and completed there; only then does the claim path come into existence, by link(2) from that temp file. A claim path that exists always names a fully written claim. There is no interval in which a concurrent starter can observe a partial claim, so the case v1 mishandled cannot arise.\n\n2. One atomic acquisition and stale-transfer protocol with exactly one winner is specified in section 3, with the interleavings walked in section 4. An incomplete or malformed claim is never treated as vacant: it is a refusal, and the refusal names the file and the manual recovery step. A loser that has already bound closes its listener before it ever serves.\n\n3. Bind order is reconciled explicitly. The listener is bound before ownership is contested, because the claim must carry the real URL and port zero is only resolved by binding. The listener is never handed to the HTTP server until ownership is held, and is closed on any loss or refusal. Ownership, not binding, is what authorizes serving.\n\n4. Every existing claim is probed conservatively, including one whose genesis differs. Genesis mismatch no longer authorizes replacement by itself. One rule now covers every case: only a definitive negative probe authorizes transfer; everything else refuses. v1's alternation between refusing and replacing on identity mismatch is gone.\n\n5. The generation field is cut. It was never stored in the claim and never compared, so it was decoration. The identity endpoint returns genesis alone. Withdrawal is tied to the claimed owner nonce and to the inode the owner created, both of which are stored and compared.\n\n6. Phase 2 documents the ownership-versus-advertisement contract in docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head. The probe reuses the strict loopback validation already in internal/residentclient/client.go:51-65, reads a bounded body, sends no credential, is a plain GET with no side effect, and the endpoint sets no CORS header. The owner nonce and fail-closed ambiguity are kept.\n\n== 3. THE PROTOCOL ==\n\nTwo files, with one authority. The ownership authority is a new claim file at MetaDir/owner. The advertisement at MetaDir/resident.json is demoted to endpoint metadata: it is written only by the process that already holds ownership, and it keeps its current shape and meaning for clients (internal/app/resident.go:55-61 is unchanged, so ResidentURL and every existing reader keep working). This is codex's permitted split, and I take it for the reason codex gave rather than the reason v1 refused it: the two records can disagree, but disagreement is harmless when only one of them confers authority and the other is refreshed by whoever holds it. Overloading one file as both lock and endpoint is what forced v1 into an unsafe single-file transfer.\n\nThe claim content is genesis, the bound URL, a fresh 16-byte random owner nonce, and the PID as a diagnostic only.\n\nACQUIRE(claim):\n  write the complete claim to MetaDir/owner.\u003cnonce\u003e.tmp and fsync it\n  link(owner.\u003cnonce\u003e.tmp, owner)\n  unlink(owner.\u003cnonce\u003e.tmp)\n  EEXIST from link means the claim is held; go to CONTEST\nlink(2) fails if the destination exists, so exactly one of any number of concurrent linkers wins, and because the content was complete before the link, the claim is complete-or-absent by construction.\n\nCONTEST:\n  read MetaDir/owner\n  absent                          -\u003e ACQUIRE\n  unreadable, short, or malformed -\u003e REFUSE (never vacant)\n  otherwise                       -\u003e PROBE its URL\n\nPROBE(url) with a short deadline, GET /v0/identity:\n  answers, genesis equals the claim's genesis   -\u003e owner is alive     -\u003e REFUSE, naming the URL\n  answers, genesis differs from the claim's     -\u003e ambiguous          -\u003e REFUSE\n  dial refused, nothing listening on that address -\u003e definitively dead -\u003e TRANSFER\n  timeout, connected but silent, unparseable    -\u003e ambiguous          -\u003e REFUSE\nThe claim's own genesis is compared against the answer, so a port inherited by an unrelated service cannot read as a live incumbent, and a claim from a workroom this directory no longer has is still probed before anything is taken from it.\n\nTRANSFER (only after a definitive negative):\n  rename(owner, owner.dead.\u003cour-nonce\u003e)\n  ENOENT -\u003e another taker or a newcomer got there first -\u003e restart CONTEST\n  success -\u003e we alone won the transfer -\u003e ACQUIRE\n    EEXIST from that ACQUIRE -\u003e a newcomer linked meanwhile -\u003e restart CONTEST\n  on success unlink owner.dead.\u003cour-nonce\u003e\nrename(2) moves the inode at a name exactly once; a second rename of the same source gets ENOENT. That is the compare-and-swap v1 lacked. The whole algorithm is bounded by a small retry count, and exhausting it refuses.\n\nSERVE: only now is the listener handed to the HTTP server, and resident.json written.\n\nWITHDRAW: stat MetaDir/owner; if its device and inode are the ones we created and its nonce is still ours, unlink it, then withdraw resident.json under the same nonce guard. Best effort by design.\n\nREFUSE at any point: close the bound listener before returning, exit non-zero, name the incumbent URL or the offending file and the recovery step.\n\nWithdrawal is deliberately an optimization, not a correctness requirement. A claim left behind by a crash costs the next starter one refused dial. The invariant that must hold is that two processes never serve one repository; a stale claim never violates it, because no claim is ever trusted as live without a probe.\n\n== 4. INTERLEAVINGS, EACH WITH ONE WINNER ==\n\nTwo simultaneous fresh starters. Both bind different ports, both write their own temp file, both attempt link. link is atomic against an existing destination, so exactly one succeeds. The loser gets EEXIST, contests, probes the winner, finds it alive, closes its listener and exits non-zero without serving. One winner. In v1 this was the case where two starters could both classify the other's in-progress file as unparseable; there is no in-progress file now.\n\nTwo takers racing a stale transfer. Both probe the same dead claim and both get a definitive negative. Both attempt rename of MetaDir/owner. One moves the inode; the other gets ENOENT and restarts CONTEST. The winner links its fresh claim and serves. The restarting taker either finds no claim and acquires (only possible if the winner has since exited) or finds the winner's claim, probes it, finds it alive and refuses. One winner.\n\nA taker racing the incumbent's clean shutdown. The incumbent closes its listener first, then withdraws. If the taker probes in between, it gets a refused dial, transfers, links and serves; the incumbent's withdrawal then stats MetaDir/owner, sees an inode and nonce that are not its own, and does nothing. If the incumbent's withdrawal runs first, the taker's CONTEST finds no claim and acquires directly. Either order yields one winner, and the nonce-and-inode guard is what stops a departing process from deleting its successor's claim.\n\nA newcomer racing a taker mid-transfer. The taker has renamed the dead claim to its tombstone, so the claim path is briefly free. A newcomer links there first. The taker's subsequent ACQUIRE gets EEXIST, restarts CONTEST, probes the newcomer, finds it alive and refuses. One winner, and the transfer winner is not privileged over an ordinary starter, which is what keeps the two paths from needing to know about each other.\n\nA crash mid-acquisition. The process died between writing its temp file and linking: the temp file is orphaned and no claim exists, so the next starter acquires normally. Orphaned temp and tombstone files are named with the owner nonce and are swept on successful acquisition.\n\n== 5. WHY link(2) RATHER THAN THE ALTERNATIVES ==\n\nO_EXCL is rejected for the reason in section 1. mkdir is atomic and fails if present, but a directory cannot carry the claim content atomically, so it reintroduces the same window one level down. An advisory lock held by an open descriptor, flock or POSIX record locking, is released automatically by the kernel on process death, which sounds attractive and is the wrong trade here: it makes liveness a property of a held descriptor rather than of a served room, it needs a platform-specific syscall the tree currently has nowhere (there is no flock in the codebase and golang.org/x/sys is only an indirect dependency), and its behaviour over network filesystems is worse than link's, not better. link(2) needs no dependency, is available on every filesystem the supported host uses, and gives the one property the primitive must have: the name appears only when the content behind it is finished.\n\nThe honest limitation is that link(2) is not reliably exclusive on some network filesystems. The supported host is a local trusted workstation or container, so this is outside the supported envelope, and phase 2 states it in the deployment documentation rather than engineering around it.\n\n== 6. PHASE-2 SHAPE ==\n\nBranch request/multi-resident-coordination, as the request records.\n\ninternal/app/resident.go gains the claim: ACQUIRE, CONTEST, TRANSFER, WITHDRAW, the owner nonce, and the inode guard. PublishResident keeps its name and meaning but becomes reachable only to an owner. cmd/gs/main.go:1654-1719 gains the ownership step between binding and serving, and the refusal messages. internal/service/server.go gains GET /v0/identity returning genesis alone. internal/residentclient/client.go carries the probe, reusing ValidateURL at client.go:51-65 and a bounded read. docs/reference/architecture.md documents the ownership-versus-advertisement contract in the same head; docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md:108-119 and docs/reference/gs/serve.md:121-129 replace \"Nothing enforces it\" with what is now enforced, the ambiguous-probe refusal, and the manual recovery step.\n\nTests: two simultaneous starters, exactly one serving; a bound loser proven never to have served; a malformed and a truncated claim each refused rather than replaced; stale-owner recovery after a definitive negative; a hung listener that accepts but never answers refused rather than taken over; a port inherited by a different genesis refused; two takers racing one stale claim with exactly one winner; a taker racing a clean shutdown in both orders; graceful shutdown releasing the claim; path aliases, symlinks and a linked worktree contending for one claim while two distinct repositories serve concurrently; and the existing kernel concurrency tests passing unchanged to show the CAS path untouched. Wire parity and public surface gates cover the new endpoint.\n\nArtifacts at the exact live strings the merge actually changes: internal/app, cmd/gs, internal/service, internal/residentclient, docs, and docs/reference/architecture.md if no wider live docs artifact covers it at merge time. Five of the six live internal/app artifacts and the live cmd/gs and internal/service ones are codex's, and the fold admits a supersession only from the author or an actor holding ratifier, so codex or hugh must retire them at step 5.\n\n== 7. WHAT I AM ASKING THE REVIEWER TO DECIDE ==\n\nWhether link(2)-plus-rename(2) is accepted as the acquisition and stale-transfer primitive; whether the four interleavings in section 4 are complete, or which one is missing; whether demoting resident.json to endpoint metadata behind a separate owner claim is the right split; whether refusing on every ambiguity, including a claim whose probe answers with an unexpected genesis, is the correct fail-closed rule; and whether cutting generation from the identity endpoint leaves anything phase 2 will need.\n\nNo implementation has begun. Phase 2 starts after this direction is approved."}